<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Holy Crown of Hungary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th–12th century</span> · Also known as Crown of Saint Stephen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-of-thorns">Holy Crown</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> is the coronation crown of the Hungarian kings, traditionally linked with Stephen I, though its surviving form combines Byzantine enamel work of the later eleventh century with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> upper part. From the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it embodied the kingdom itself, and a king was not fully legitimate until crowned with it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szekesfehervar">Székesfehérvár</a>. Since 2000 it has been displayed in the Hungarian Parliament building.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/holy-crown-of-hungary.jpg" alt="Holy Crown of Hungary" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: granada_turnier · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hungarian_Parliament_002_-_Flickr_-_granada_turnier.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
